wineoss: tee what reaches the device, because every layer above it is eliminated.

Requiem buzzes. Metro does not. Both go through this driver, on this device, and
the two traces are statistically the same driver doing the same thing:

    burst / capacity / client buffer   868 / 1736 / 2604   in both
    held   (client data waiting)       mean 1870  vs 1909
    in_oss (queued in the device)      mean 1106  vs 1144
    partial writes                     41%        vs 40%
    AAudio xrun count                  0          vs 0

Neither end starves; the device never reported an underrun; the partial-write
path is correct (`in_oss_frames` advances by what AAudio accepted, not by what
was offered, and 40% partial turns out to be ordinary). Requiem does not even
reach the float-to-i16 conversion — it asks for int16 and gets it.

So the frames arriving here are already wrong, and **no counter can say in what
way**. That is what this patch is for.

FOUR THEORIES DIED BEFORE IT, and they are listed so none is retried:

  * breadcrumb instrumentation starving the audio thread -- the buzz survived
    removing it;
  * the 24 fps limit starving the buffer -- the timer loop tops up every 10 ms
    regardless of frame rate, so the frame interval never enters into it;
  * partial writes dropping samples -- see above, and Metro partials identically;
  * relaxed memory ordering corrupting Wwise's SIMD mixing -- closing all three
    of FEX's remaining knobs did not change the buzz and stopped the title
    reaching a frame at all. Recorded at STRICT_MEMORY_ORDERING.

Three failures sound alike and want different fixes, which is the argument for a
capture rather than another counter: broadband noise says the samples are being
*computed* wrong, periodic gaps or repeats say the guest's mixer is missing its
deadline, and flat-topped peaks say something is clipping. One look at a
waveform separates them. No amount of line counting will.

Off unless `VESSEL_AUDIO_DUMP` is set. Opened once, appended to, and never
retried after a failed open -- a diagnostic that could itself fail a stream would
be worse than the bug it is chasing.

`1`, `on` or `true` means "somewhere the container owns", and that spelling is
the whole reason this is usable from the Diagnostics screen: the container's
scratch directory has a UUID in it, so a user asked to type an absolute path
could not know one, and a row that can only be filled in wrongly is not a
control. Three spellings because three layers write it — the Diagnostics row
sends `on`, a script sends `1`. Any other value is taken as a literal path,
which is what a shell session wants; `0`, `off`, `false` and the empty string
are off, so the row has a real Off.

Raw interleaved frames, in whatever format the stream negotiated, with the rate,
channel count and sample width on the TRACE line that reports the open. No WAV
header deliberately: a header written by the driver currently under suspicion for
producing wrong samples is one more thing to have to trust, and `ffmpeg -f s16le
-ar <rate> -ac <ch>` costs nothing to type.

Both write sites are teed. The second is the wrap case -- the tail of the ring
written after the head -- and a capture missing it would show a periodic
discontinuity that the device never heard, which is exactly the artefact this is
meant to identify.

diff --git a/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c b/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c
--- a/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c
+++ b/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c
@@ -40,6 +40,9 @@
 #endif
 
 #include <stdarg.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
@@ -906,6 +909,84 @@ static void handle_disconnect(struct oss_stream *stream)
         stream->reopen_backoff = DISCONNECT_RETRY_PASSES;
 }
 
+/* Vessel: tee what is actually handed to the device, so a buzz can be looked at.
+ *
+ * **Every layer above the samples has been eliminated and the buzz survived.**
+ * Requiem buzzes, Metro does not, and the two traces are statistically the same
+ * driver: burst 868, capacity 1736, client buffer 2604 in both; `held` mean 1870
+ * against 1909; `in_oss` 1106 against 1144; 41% against 40% partial writes; and
+ * zero AAudio xruns in either. Neither end starves. So the frames arriving here
+ * are already wrong, and no counter can say in what way.
+ *
+ * Three failures sound alike and want different fixes, which is the whole reason
+ * this exists: broadband noise says the samples are being computed wrong
+ * (miscompiled DSP), periodic gaps or repeats say the guest's mixer is missing
+ * its deadline, and flat-topped peaks say something is clipping. A capture tells
+ * them apart in one look; a line count never will.
+ *
+ * Off unless `VESSEL_AUDIO_DUMP` is set, opened once, appended to, and never
+ * checked again after a failed open -- a diagnostic that could itself fail a
+ * stream would be worse than the bug. Raw interleaved frames in whatever format
+ * the stream negotiated, which the header line records, because a WAV header
+ * written by a driver that is being blamed for wrong samples is one more thing
+ * to doubt. */
+static void vessel_audio_dump(struct oss_stream *stream, const void *buf, UINT32 frames)
+{
+    static int fd = -2;   /* -2 unopened, -1 disabled */
+
+    if(fd == -1)
+        return;
+
+    if(fd == -2){
+        const char *path = getenv("VESSEL_AUDIO_DUMP");
+        char fallback[1024];
+
+        if(!path || !*path || !strcmp(path, "0") || !strcmp(path, "off") ||
+           !strcmp(path, "false")){
+            fd = -1;
+            return;
+        }
+
+        /* A switch, not a path. The container's scratch directory has a UUID in
+         * it, so a settings screen cannot name it and a user asked to type it
+         * could not know it -- and a control that can only be filled in wrongly
+         * is worse than no control. TMPDIR is that directory and is cleared when
+         * the container is reset. Three spellings because three layers write
+         * this: the Diagnostics row sends `on`, a script sends `1`, and `true`
+         * costs nothing to accept. Anything else is a literal path, which is
+         * what a shell session wants. */
+        if(!strcmp(path, "1") || !strcmp(path, "on") || !strcmp(path, "true")){
+            const char *tmp = getenv("TMPDIR");
+
+            snprintf(fallback, sizeof(fallback), "%s/vessel-audio.pcm",
+                     tmp && *tmp ? tmp : "/tmp");
+            path = fallback;
+        }
+
+        fd = open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0644);
+        if(fd < 0){
+            WARN("audio dump: cannot open %s: %s\n", path, strerror(errno));
+            fd = -1;
+            return;
+        }
+        TRACE("audio dump: %s, %u Hz, %u ch, %u bits, block %u\n", path,
+              (unsigned int)stream->fmt->nSamplesPerSec,
+              (unsigned int)stream->fmt->nChannels,
+              (unsigned int)stream->fmt->wBitsPerSample,
+              (unsigned int)stream->fmt->nBlockAlign);
+    }
+
+    if(frames){
+        /* The return is deliberately ignored and deliberately not retried. A
+         * short write loses samples the device did hear, which is a hole in the
+         * capture and not a fault in the stream; a retry loop here would put the
+         * audio thread's deadline in the hands of a diagnostic file. */
+        ssize_t ignored = write(fd, buf, frames * stream->fmt->nBlockAlign);
+
+        (void)ignored;
+    }
+}
+
 static void oss_write_data(struct oss_stream *stream)
 {
     UINT32 written_frames, in_oss_frames, write_limit, max_period, write_offs_frames, new_frames;
@@ -1007,6 +1088,7 @@ static void oss_write_data(struct oss_stream *stream)
     if(stream->convert_to_i16 && !(buf = (BYTE *)convert_to_i16_frames(stream, buf, to_write_frames)))
         return;
 
+    vessel_audio_dump(stream, buf, to_write_frames);
     written = AAudioStream_write(stream->aas, buf, to_write_frames, 0);
     if(written < 0){
         /* A disconnect is recoverable and nothing else here is: an argument
@@ -1086,7 +1168,8 @@ static void oss_write_data(struct oss_stream *stream)
         if(stream->convert_to_i16 && !(buf = (BYTE *)convert_to_i16_frames(stream, buf, to_write_frames)))
             return;
 
-        written = AAudioStream_write(stream->aas, buf, to_write_frames, 0);
+        vessel_audio_dump(stream, buf, to_write_frames);
+        written = AAudioStream_write(stream->aas, buf, to_write_frames, 0);
         if(written < 0){
             if(written == AAUDIO_ERROR_DISCONNECTED)
                 handle_disconnect(stream);
